INT. TOLLIVER HOUSE - KITCHEN - MORNING

Sunlight on yellow linoleum. A clock shaped like a rooster. A pot of coffee on the stove, a stack of unopened mail.

GLORIA MENDEZ-HART, 60s, in a floral housecoat, cracks eggs into a bowl with practiced flicks. RAYMOND TOLLIVER, 60s, her husband, reads the newspaper at the table, spectacles low. CLAIRE TOLLIVER, 30s, their daughter, stands in the doorway with a suitcase in one hand and her car keys in the other.

GLORIA
You're up early.

CLAIRE
I couldn't sleep.

GLORIA
Sit. I'll make you eggs.

CLAIRE
I'm not hungry, Mama.

GLORIA
Nonsense. You're a Tolliver. You're always hungry.

RAYMOND
(from behind the paper)
She's got a point.

CLAIRE
Daddy.

RAYMOND
I'm just agreeing with your mother. It's rare, so savour it.

Claire sets the suitcase down by the door. She hesitates. Then she sits at the table across from him, still holding her keys.

GLORIA
Toast?

CLAIRE
Sure.

GLORIA
Butter or jam?

CLAIRE
Both.

GLORIA
That's my girl.

She drops two slices into the toaster. Hums. Raymond turns a page. The clock ticks. A fly buzzes against the window. It is, in every way, an ordinary morning.

RAYMOND
How's the drive?

CLAIRE
Six hours.

RAYMOND
Take the highway. The back road's a mess.

CLAIRE
I know.

RAYMOND
Stop for gas in Salina.

CLAIRE
Daddy, I know.

RAYMOND
I'm only saying.

GLORIA
He's only saying.

CLAIRE
Mama.

GLORIA
What?

CLAIRE
Nothing.

The toast pops. Gloria sets a plate in front of her daughter, jam spread thick, butter melting. Claire stares at it.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
I need to tell you something.

GLORIA
You always say that before you tell us something awful.

CLAIRE
It isn't awful.

RAYMOND
(lowering the paper)
Then say it.

CLAIRE
I'm not going to Chicago.

Silence. The rooster clock ticks.

GLORIA
Of course you are. You've got the apartment.

CLAIRE
I gave it up.

RAYMOND
When?

CLAIRE
Two weeks ago.

GLORIA
Two weeks.

CLAIRE
I didn't know how to tell you.

RAYMOND
What about the job?

CLAIRE
I quit.

GLORIA
(sharply)
You QUIT?

CLAIRE
Mama, please.

GLORIA
You studied for six years.

CLAIRE
I know.

GLORIA
We paid for--

RAYMOND
Gloria.

GLORIA
What? I'm asking.

RAYMOND
Let her talk.

GLORIA
Claire. Talk.

Claire folds her hands on the table. She looks first at her father, then at her mother, then at the plate of toast. Her voice, when it comes, is small and steady.

CLAIRE
I'm sick.

Gloria's whisk stops. Raymond's spectacles slide down his nose.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
I didn't want to say it until I was sure. Now I'm sure. I'm sorry.

GLORIA
(barely audible)
Claire.

CLAIRE
That's why I came home. To tell you in the kitchen. I thought... I don't know. I thought the kitchen would be easier.

RAYMOND
(a long moment)
Is it the kind they fix?

CLAIRE
They think so.

RAYMOND
Then that's what we'll do.

GLORIA
(setting down the whisk)
We will.

CLAIRE
Mama--

GLORIA
We will.

She crosses the room, and takes her daughter's face in both her hands. The coffee boils over behind her. Nobody moves to stop it.
